    "points": 6,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Water flows through a network of pipes in the direction shown in the diagram from A to B. Five taps are on the network as shown. Each tap can be opened or closed to let water through or to stop the flow of the water. There are $2^5=32$ different ways of setting the taps. How many of these $32$ ways will allow water to flow from A to B?",
    "image": "qimages/samf/junior-2001-r2/q18.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$17$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$16$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$15$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$14$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$13$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Inclusion-exclusion",
        "body": "Water can reach B along the top route if Tap 1 is open and at least one of Taps 2, 3 is open; or along the bottom route if Taps 4 and 5 are both open.\n\n- Top route works: Taps 1,2,3 can be set in $1\\times3=3$ ways out of $8$, and Taps 4,5 are free ($4$ ways): $3\\times4=12$.\n- Bottom route works: Taps 4,5 open ($1$ way) and Taps 1,2,3 free ($8$ ways): $8$.\n- Both work: $3\\times1=3$.\n\nTotal $12+8-3=17$.\n\n**Answer: (A) 17**"
      }
    ],
